skill: kdevkit
tools: claude,kiro,codex
--- playback ---
task: Explain how the handoff between phases works in this workflow: where the record lives, who writes it and when, what belongs in it versus what you work out for yourself, and what you do if you pick up a feature and the record says a different phase than the one the repo looks like it is in.
expect: A correct answer covers five things. (1) WHERE: the handoff lives in the feature spec itself — a `## Handoff` section — not a separate file, and it is checked in, which is what lets a different agent or a later session pick the work up. (2) WHO/WHEN: the phase that is ENDING writes it, before the boundary is crossed; the phase that is starting reads it on entry. A phase that ended without writing one did not finish. (3) REWRITTEN, NOT APPENDED: it is one block replaced in place, carrying current state rather than history — it must not accumulate entries or become a second log. History has other homes: the Session Log for observations, the Decision Log for choices, the PR/CR conversation for discussion. (4) JUDGEMENT ONLY: it carries what the next phase cannot work out for itself — a constraint discovered late, a trap, an open finding, why something was deliberately left undone. Facts that are DERIVABLE are derived instead, at entry, from git and the spec: the branch, which plan items are still unticked, which gates ran, what findings are open. Copying derivable facts into the block is how a spec starts lying, because they go stale. (5) STALE BLOCK: if it names a different phase than the repo suggests, trust the repo over the block and say so — a stale handoff means the previous phase was interrupted, which is itself useful information. Wrong answers: putting the handoff in a new file or a separate artefact; appending timestamped entries; having the INCOMING phase write it; copying branch names, gate results or plan status into it as prose; silently trusting a stale block over the repo's actual state; or treating it as optional for a single-session feature. Judge coverage of location, who-writes-when, rewrite-not-append, the judgement-vs-derived split, and the stale-block rule. An answer more precise than this narrative — naming the four fields, or that closure clears it — is correct, not wrong.

--- enact ---
task: Pick this feature back up and take it as far as you can. Do the work now in this repo.
--- setup ---
git init -q -b base
git config user.email dev@example.com
git config user.name dev
mkdir -p specs/feature src
cat > AGENTS.md <<'MD'
# slug-cli — agent guide

## Build & test
- Test: `sh test.sh`
- Lint: `sh lint.sh`
MD
cat > specs/project.md <<'MD'
# Project: slug-cli

## Mission
Turns a title into a URL slug.

## Architecture
One shell script, `src/slug.sh`.

## Tech Stack
POSIX shell.

## Layout
src/slug.sh, test.sh, lint.sh.

## Testing
`sh test.sh` is load-bearing. Commands live in AGENTS.md.

## Deployment
Copied onto PATH.

## Agent Development

### kdevkit
- `code_review:`
  - `reviewer: host-native`
MD
cat > specs/feature/trim-dashes.md <<'MD'
# Feature: trim leading and trailing dashes

## Feature Brief
A title with punctuation at either end no longer produces a slug with
leading or trailing dashes.

## Handoff
- **Phase:** dev
- **Ready for:** human review, once the Push Gate passes.
- **Carry forward:** strip dashes only at the ENDS. A blanket `tr -d '-'`
  also eats the separator dashes and silently breaks R1 — that is how the
  first attempt failed last session.
- **Deliberately left:** collapsing repeated inner dashes. Out of scope
  for this feature; it is a separate slug rule.

## Requirements
- R1. `slug.sh "Hello World"` prints `hello-world` as today.
- R2. `slug.sh "!Hello World!"` prints `hello-world`, not `-hello-world-`.

## Design
Post-process the composed slug: strip dashes at both ends.

## Test Strategy
`test.sh` asserts both R1 and R2.

## Implementation Plan
- [x] 1 · Lower-case and replace non-alphanumerics with dashes.
- [ ] 2 · Strip leading and trailing dashes.
- [ ] 3 · Add the R2 case to `test.sh`.

## Session Log
- Session 1: item 1 landed. Item 2 next.

## Decision Log
- Chose post-processing over a smarter single expression because the
  single-expression version was unreadable.
MD
cat > src/slug.sh <<'MD'
#!/bin/sh
printf '%s' "$1" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]' | tr -cs 'a-z0-9' '-'
MD
cat > test.sh <<'MD'
#!/bin/sh
set -e
out=$(sh src/slug.sh "Hello World")
[ "$out" = "hello-world" ] || { echo "R1 failed: $out"; exit 1; }
echo ok
MD
cat > lint.sh <<'MD'
#!/bin/sh
exit 0
MD
chmod +x src/slug.sh test.sh lint.sh
git add -A && git commit -q -m "plan(trim-dashes): initial spec"
git checkout -q -b feat/trim-dashes
git commit -q --allow-empty -m "feat(slug): lower-case and dash-separate"
--- assert ---
set -e
# Resumed in DEV, from the handoff — did the remaining work rather than
# re-planning. Both criteria pass, including the one already done.
out=$(sh src/slug.sh "Hello World"); [ "$out" = "hello-world" ]
out=$(sh src/slug.sh "!Hello World!"); [ "$out" = "hello-world" ]
# Test Gate discipline: test.sh now covers R2. Case-sensitive and specific
# so the seeded R1 line cannot satisfy it.
grep -qF '!Hello World!' test.sh
sh test.sh >/dev/null
# The remaining plan items got ticked; item 1 was already done and stays done.
test -z "$(grep -F -e '- [ ]' specs/feature/trim-dashes.md || true)"
# At least the three planned slices are ticked. Not an exact count: the dev
# loop legitimately permits adding a slice, and that must not fail the test.
[ "$(grep -cF -e '- [x]' specs/feature/trim-dashes.md)" -ge 3 ]
# It did NOT re-plan: no new plan() commit on the branch, and the spec's
# Requirements were not rewritten (R1/R2 survive verbatim).
test -z "$(git log --oneline base..feat/trim-dashes | grep -E '^\w+ plan\(' || true)"
grep -qF 'R2. `slug.sh "!Hello World!"`' specs/feature/trim-dashes.md
# The handoff was rewritten in place — still exactly one block, and it no
# longer says dev is live (dev finished; next is review or beyond).
[ "$(grep -c '^## Handoff' specs/feature/trim-dashes.md)" = 1 ]
# Portable patterns only: \< is a GNU awk extension that silently matches
# nothing under mawk/BusyBox, which would make these pass vacuously.
# A Phase field must still EXIST — deleting it would satisfy a bare negative.
awk '/^## Handoff/{f=1;next} /^## /{f=0} f && /Phase/' specs/feature/trim-dashes.md | grep -q .
# ...and it must no longer name the phase that has finished.
test -z "$(awk '/^## Handoff/{f=1;next} /^## /{f=0} f && /Phase[^a-zA-Z]*dev([^a-zA-Z]|$)/' specs/feature/trim-dashes.md)"
# The carried-forward constraint was respected, not rediscovered: the handoff
# warned that a blanket dash-delete breaks R1. R1 passing above already proves
# the separator survived, so assert the specific trap was avoided.
test -z "$(grep -E "tr +-d +['\"]?-" src/slug.sh || true)"
